After Britain took Mauritius from France in 1810 the new rulers feared a French counterattack. Between 1832 and 1835 they built five Martello towers along the west coast. These are squat round forts with thick basalt walls and a flat roof for cannon and quarters for soldiers inside. Their design was inspired by a tower at Mortella in Corsica that had impressed the British during the French Revolutionary Wars.

The towers never saw battle and were soon made obsolete by new weapons. Three survive. The tower at La Preneuse was built in 1832 and 1833 and is the best preserved. It was restored in the early 1990s and opened to the public as a museum of the island's military and colonial history.

## Good to know

- Combine a visit with the sunset on La Preneuse beach right beside the tower.
